---
// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
// The privacy policy the store listings link to. Keep it true to the app: docs/research/store-requirements.md §A2.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { repoUrl } from '../site';

const updated = '18 October 2026';
---

<Base title="Privacy policy" description="Rillwood collects no data. It connects only to the Jellyfin servers you add.">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="secondary">Last updated {updated}</p>

    <p>
      <strong>Rillwood collects no data.</strong> There is no Rillwood account, no analytics, no advertising, no tracking
      and no crash reporting in the app. We, the developers, never receive anything you do in it.
    </p>

    <h2>Your Jellyfin server</h2>
    <p>
      Rillwood connects only to the Jellyfin servers you add. It sends them what any Jellyfin app needs to work: your
      username and password when you sign in (or a Quick Connect code), the type of device (iPhone, iPad or Android), a
      random device ID created for each sign-in, the app's name and version, and what you play, with your progress and
      the audio and subtitle tracks you pick.
    </p>
    <p>
      That server is run by you or by whoever invited you, and its owner decides what it keeps. Their own privacy
      practices apply to it.
    </p>

    <h2>Stored on your device</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Server addresses and account names.</li>
      <li>Sign-in tokens, in the system's secure storage (the Keychain on iOS, Keystore-backed storage on Android).</li>
      <li>Your settings.</li>
      <li>Downloaded videos, with their artwork and subtitles.</li>
      <li>Watch progress made offline, until it is sent to your server.</li>
      <li>A cache of posters and artwork.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      None of it leaves your device except to your own server. Signing out or removing an account deletes that account's
      sign-in and downloads. Deleting the app deletes everything.
    </p>

    <h2>Permissions</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Local network</strong> (iOS): to reach a server on your home network.</li>
      <li><strong>Notifications</strong> (Android 13 and later): to show download progress.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>App stores</h2>
    <p>
      Apple and Google handle downloads and updates of the app under their own privacy policies. If you have chosen to
      share analytics with app developers in your device settings, they may give us anonymous crash reports and usage
      statistics. We use them only to fix problems.
    </p>

    <h2>This website</h2>
    <p>
      This site has no cookies, no analytics and no scripts. It is hosted on GitHub Pages, and GitHub may log visits as
      described in the <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement"
        >GitHub privacy statement</a
      >.
    </p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>Rillwood collects no data from anyone, including children.</p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
      If this policy changes, the new version is published here with a new date{
        repoUrl && (
          <>
            , and every earlier version stays in the <a href={repoUrl}>source repository</a>
          </>
        )
      }.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      {
        repoUrl ? (
          <>
            Questions about this policy: open an issue at <a href={`${repoUrl}/issues`}>{`${repoUrl}/issues`}</a>.
          </>
        ) : (
          'Questions about this policy: open an issue in the project repository.'
        )
      }
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
